+{"A": "A room in the sun", "B": "A room, empty of life and filled with the presence of light that could not be extinguished.\n\nWithin its confines, shadows were cast in the direction opposite to where they fell when it was dark.\n\nInside this chamber, every action had meaning; each word spoken echoed off walls covered by layers of dust - as if seeking shelter from a storm or protection against raindrops falling onto their faces during an unending winter morning before sunrise dawned upon them.", "C": "A room in the sun", "form": null, "id": "p02", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about a locked room filling with sunlight"}
+{"A": "You don't recognize me\n\nI can hear your laughter,\n\nbut I can't remember it, or if you're even there.\n\nBut every time someone asks me to make up stories again,\n\nI say, \"No.\"\n\nBecause my mind's a blank as the night sky.\n\nAnd when I turn to you for comfort,\nit doesn’t feel like we’re in sync anymore.\n\nIt’s just the world looking through our eyes.\n\nLike something else was meant to be.\n\nSo that one day I’ll know what happened.\n\nWhen everything comes undone and forgotten.\n\nNow my heart’s on fire, but nothing feels real.\n\nI’m scared of this feeling inside because I don’t understand why.\n\n\n# You don't recognize me", "B": "You don't recognize me in the crowd,\nand if you do, it's because I'm a stranger to you too.\n\nI walk down the street with my hair down,\ntrying to figure out who you are and what this is all about.\nCan we start over again?\nBecause even though I know you're here,\nit doesn't feel like I've been waiting for you.\n\nWe used to be so close,\nbut now things have changed so much that they make no sense at all.\n\nDo you remember when we used to play hide-and-seek?\nWhen your smile was brighter than any star on the horizon?\n\nDon't you miss us? Don't you wanna come back home?\nYeah, yeah, yes, baby - give me that hand.\n\nOh, how could we forget each other just like", "C": "You don't recognize me in the crowd,\nand if you do, it's because I'm a stranger to you.\n\nMy name is Alex,\n\nBut you'll never know what it means.\n\nI've been looking for answers in my mind,\nhoping they'd show up somewhere along the way.\n\nAnd now that I finally have them,\nit's time for us both to take a walk away.\n\nThere was this one time when we were kids together,\nand our friendship was so sweet and true.\n\nNow here we are twenty years later,\nbut there's no sign of change or growth between us anymore.\n\nWe started off with just two people who believed in everything together,\nbut sadly, things got lost as time went on.\n\nSo tell me how long can I keep seeing shadows in your life?\nWhat could", "form": null, "id": "p03", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about not recognizing an old friend"}
+{"A": "A few drops of rain fell on the glass roof, which was covered in blue paint. The doctor took off his hat and wiped it with an old cloth.\n\n“Please tell me what’s wrong?”\n\nHe asked, shaking his head as if he was waiting for an answer from someone else.\n\n\nThe girl nodded once she noticed how good her hair looked after wiping it dry with water. She took her scarf out of her pocket so that her hair could be washed more effectively.\n\n\n\nYou can", "B": "A few days later, the sun is shining over the sea,\nbut in the hospital it’s dark and cold.\n\nThe patient, who has been ill for years, \nhas not returned home since her illness struck.\nShe feels as if she’s lost everything; there are no friends to talk with anymore.\nShe can’t eat or sleep without thinking about all the bad things that happen every day.\n\nThe staff nurse stays by the bed until the end of the night.\nThey try to", "C": "A few drops of rain fell on the glass roof, which was covered in blue paint. The doctor took off his hat and wiped it with an old cloth.\n\n“Please tell me what’s wrong?”\n\nHe asked, shaking his head as if he was waiting for an answer from someone else.\n\n\nThe girl nodded once she noticed how good her hair looked after wiping it dry with water. She took her scarf out of her pocket so that her hair could be washed more effectively.\n\n\n\nYou can", "form": null, "id": "p04", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about rain inside a hospital"}
+{"A": "It’s a simple case of not wanting to share, the desire for more than what you already have. There are times when our heart feels jealous but we don’t say anything. It’s something in your chest that makes you feel bad inside.", "B": "It’s a simple thing, is jealousy,\nand yet the world seems to forget what we’re trying to say.\nWhat you did was right and wrong all at once,\nAnd this little heart of mine knows it better than any man or woman could.\n\nI’ll leave it at that", "C": "A woman stood in the kitchen, her hands moving swiftly over the counter. She was surrounded by food—tasty treats and familiar aromas that made her smile.\n\nHer partner sat across from her, a look of confusion on his face. He knew she loved him with all her heart but wasn't entirely sure what to do next.\n\nShe took another bite, tasting something new every time; salted almonds coated with honey for an explosion of flavors. Their laughter echoed through the house", "form": null, "id": "p07", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about jealousy without naming it"}
+{"A": "I don’t want you to walk away from me\nI’ll be alright by myself, I’m not a coward\nAnd the feeling is in your heart too\nYou can see where we were at before\nWe’re back to where it started again\nI know what’s going on around us now\nThere was never anything wrong\nBut every time I get scared\nIt doesn't mean that I'm afraid of life anymore\n\nWe used to be so lost\nNow we're just confused and blind\nI wanna go some place far away \nLike the people who are free\n\nThe things they told us about being crazy \nThe things we've been taught to say or do\nThey were telling us how to live our lives\nThat we could make any dream come true\nYeah there's no one else for me", "B": "I don’t want you to walk away from me\nI’ll be alright by myself, I’m fine, no one cares that much\nBut then again, how can someone really know what they need?\nOr even why do we love the things that hurt us?\n\nThere is a lot of love in my heart,\nIt’s something hard to explain,\nYou’re supposed to live out on your own,\nThen there will be an ending.\n\nIf it had been written down like this before\nI’d’ve known where to start,\nThe first thing I did when we left was get lost,\nWe both fell in love with each other as far as possible.\n\nI don’t wanna leave ya, but if it were up to me\nTo not say goodbye would be easier than ever,\nI won’t lie, I’ll make it through all alone,\nAnd if I could change anything at any point or time\n\n‘Cause sometimes it feels more comfortable for me\nWhen I feel like staying longer,\nWhat about now? What are we gonna do today?\nJust let go of everything you hold dear and try to keep it all safe inside,\n\nSo what else am I going to tell ya? (Yay)\n\nWhy", "C": "I don’t know if you can tell from my writing, but I really hate this place. It’s too cold in here.\n\nThat is not the best way to describe it now that we have air conditioning… though as soon as they shut off those stupid windows at night so I won’t wake up with a cough…\n\nYou were right, man. We’re supposed to be out on some kind of vacation. But damnit all… why are we staying?\n\nThe room was like an echo chamber, reflecting everything from the light outside. The fluorescent light bulbs flickering slowly through their cycle just after nine o’clock. My watch blinked 10:28.\n\nOh fuck, what did I do? I should’ve gotten up earlier. Fuck.\n\nYeah. Yeah, alright, shit. Just take your time.\n\nFuck! Don’t let me make it easy for her!\n\nShe’d already given you plenty of chances. You could say nothing.\n\nWhat am I saying? If she gives you another chance, then don’t fucking take it!\n\nHe’d said it again before he left. And even after I promised myself there would be no more lies, the truth didn’t feel possible anymore.\n\nWhy", "form": null, "id": "p15", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about wanting to leave and wanting to stay"}
+{"A": "A tower tall, and proud,\nits light never fails,\na beacon in the night,\nit guides our way to stay.\n\nWe stand high above the sea,\nour vigil through the day,\nthe sound of waves around us all,\nno more fear for harm we'll bear.\n\nThe wind's soft caress is ours,\nthis ancient stone can't fall down,\nin its shadow where we wait,\nfor that guiding star of dawn.", "B": "A tower of light, high above the sea,\nIt stands alone, like a lonely sentry;\nA beacon of hope to those who have lost their way,\nIts bright beams guide us on our endless voyage.\n\nThe wind whispers secrets in its echoing walls,\nAs it carries tales of distant lands and fears;\nThrough every storm that tempts the soul away,\nThis lighthouse remains steadfast, strong and true.\n\nWith each passing moment, the world transforms so strangely,\nAnd I wonder if", "C": "- no dialogue\n- make it feel like a true story\n\n\nWhat happened when the weather was perfect?\n\nA fog shrouded town.\n\nAnd on the top of that cliff,\na lighthouse, just sitting there,\n\nSo what happened in those days,\nwhen the rain started falling hard?", "form": null, "id": "p18", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about a lighthouse far from water"}
+{"A": "He drives slow, trying to figure out why she's upset with him.\nShe doesn't respond at all when he tries talking to her.\n\nHe wants it to be easy and natural but his words fall like thorns on the ground. \nThe silence has become uncomfortable for him so he finally speaks again... \n\n\"Can I ask you something?\"", "B": "He drives slow, trying to figure out why she's so angry.\n\nShe looks at him from the side mirror and says, \"We have a fight?\"\n\nThe window of his car is covered with fog like an ugly blanket. The cold air bites his nose but he doesn't move away from her yet because she still has both hands on the steering wheel which gives off no sound or indication whatsoever of the slightest feeling in his heart. He knows that if they weren’t there together now he'd be dead; it’s not as though he's some kind of superhuman being able to survive anything.\n\n“I'm just tired,” he lies down his arms next to her head after waiting for her to finish speaking.\n\nIt does nothing but make her angrier than before and she leans forward even further until their faces", "C": "He drives slow, trying to figure out why she's upset with him.\nShe doesn't respond at all when he tries talking to her.\n\nHe wants it to be easy and natural but his words fall like thorns on the ground. \nThe silence has become uncomfortable for him so he finally speaks again... \n\n\"Can I ask you something?\"", "form": null, "id": "p19", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a silent argument in a car"}
+{"A": "I was born on a Tuesday,\nand I’m still here.\n\nThe roof of my head\nIs like an attic, full of secrets and treasures.\nAll your favorite books,\nThey’re all in there too.\nIt’s really hard to get into this place,\nAnd no one knows how old it is—so that means\nThere might be some things you don’t want me to know.\n\nSo I will not tell any more stories,\nIf we’re going to sit around and talk about places we can't see,\nWhat do you mean by “secret place?”\nMaybe someday someone will find out why she died, but they won’t have to stay dead forever...\n\nBut when I was little and nothing could ever go wrong,\nit's now time for us to make love...\nAnd who needs light? It's just as easy if we're sleeping beneath the stars...", "B": "I was born on a Tuesday,\nand I’m still here.\n\nThe world was young then, and we were happy,\nthough in my mind I know now\nwe’d never have stayed so much longer.\n\nThere are stories of times that they said\nthis place wasn’t right for me,\nbut I’ll always believe it’s true:\n\nYou’re all grown up now, but you’re not really old yet.\nSome things won’t change no matter how many years pass.\nWe’ve come to make our own way, \neven if there is no tomorrow at all.", "C": "I was born on a Tuesday,\nand I’m still here.\n\nThe roof of my house\nIs like an attic, it’s so high;\nAnd there are things hidden in it,\nlike me.\n\nThey’ve put my name right at the bottom\nUnder the stairs and the floorboards,\nSo don’t you think that they’re hiding things too?\n\nThere are secrets underneath the ground,\nThings that we can never find,\nBut when the door opens up inside,\nIt makes our hearts start to bleed.\n\nWhat lies beneath this thin blue sky?\nWhy do all these things feel so strange?", "form": null, "id": "p20", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about something buried under the floor"}
+{"A": "The light was off.\nThat is how you knew that they had turned away.\n\nThey left before we could even see them anymore.", "B": "The light was off, the stage silent.\n\nOne woman looked up to see her fellow singers and friends looking down at them in horror.\n\n\nIt's all too much", "C": "The light was off.\nThat is how you knew that they had turned away.\n\nThey left before we could even see them anymore.", "form": null, "id": "p21", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about the exact moment a crowd turns away"}
+{"A": "The light was so much stronger here that it almost hurt your eyes, though you felt no pain. You stepped into another room and looked down at all those passengers staring back up to you with this blank expression on their face.\n\nThere were six of them now; three standing up in rows across from you and two sitting cross-legged together leaning against the wall.\n\nYou didn't know what you wanted to say but just then something came out anyway:\n\n\"Hello?\"\n\nThey ignored it for a while before one of them finally spoke up as if he had heard nothing:\n\n\"What's there to be said?\"", "B": "The light was so much stronger here\nNo one else would be out for long, it's been that way since forever.\n\nYou've forgotten what time is\nWhat I want to say is this - you're too far gone\n\nI'm waiting in the dark now\nWaiting until you get back to me \nThat's all I ever want\n\nCan't even make my cry no more\nMy soul doesn't care anymore\nThis love is over\nAnd I'll never forgive\nIt just ain't worth nothing\nLet go before you leave\n\nAll I can see is endless night", "C": "The light was so much stronger here\nNo one else would be out for long, it's been that way since forever.\n\nYou've forgotten what time is\nWhat happens when you stop believing in me?\n\nIf I tell you something you will know nothing,\nIt won't matter how strong we're feeling together\n\nThat part of your brain that tells us to love you like someone who'll take care of you never came through\nThis has been an accident with no outcome, just pure chance", "form": null, "id": "p25", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a train station after the last train"}
+{"A": "I am thinking of this photograph that has a picture of my mom on the left side and nothing on the right, but they both are just a headshot. I have no idea what to say about it unless you could tell me something from your experience or thoughts when looking at an image like this. It's so odd because it seems like someone took 3 photos in a row instead of two then stacked them together, which is really weird", "B": "I am thinking of this photograph that has just been removed from my phone, it was shot by the artist named Sven and I think I can recognise some faces in the people's masks but for some reason they are all gone.\n\nYou see there were two women in their 70s in their mid-twenties and the man had his wife's hair colour on him as well (although he didn't have any woman or children).\n\nBut where is everyone else?\n\nWhat if someone had come along after taking out the photo to remove them? What would happen then? Would you be allowed to go back to the gallery and find your own way home?", "C": "I am thinking of it now, how the whole thing started and what went wrong in there. How all those people were standing on that stage. I was among them and I know that if they’d be alive right now, I would love to see my father again. But he’s gone too. It seems like it just keeps happening to me.\n\nWhen the show started, they asked everyone who didn’t have any photos for their families or friends to come up here and we did exactly that. Then when we came down off the stage, our mothers said it was time to go home, but not without taking our children from us first and showing them how beautiful life could be. And then afterwards, no one even wanted to talk to me anymore after that last night at the memorial service because they had", "form": null, "id": "p30", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a photograph with one face missing"}
+{"A": "a small kitten is watching a car drive by on the highway", "B": "Sitting behind a car, one eye open,\nBark's watch in hand with no need to ask;\nIn my life I will not be moved.\nWatched her when she was born and so then too\nWhen she was going through it all alone,\nWatching her until she finally died.\n\nThe whole world saw me just once as we ran away\nNow that you've found your own path you must make up your mind\nYou might know this girl but never knew she'd run far away\nThis doggie sees the man who sits on top of me", "C": "a small kitten, half-way between a woman and her car,\nwatching it with its whole being.\n\nIt moves slowly forward, in front of them.\nThe car's driver is going too fast for him to be able to follow it\nwithout falling behind or getting left alone on the road ahead.\nHe keeps his head up so that he can watch what they do next; what he might do next,\nand how far he might go before he knows where he stands at all.\n\nAs if by magic,\nhe sees himself disappear into the trees after a moment, becoming part of their movement;\nthe world of cars passing over roads\nbecoming empty again,\n\nfor the most part; but not entirely -\nthere will always be something beyond us,\nsomething we never had thought possible because there are limits everywhere,", "form": null, "id": "p32", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about an animal watching from the road"}
+{"A": "I found myself in an alley, the sound of rain on asphalt echoing through my mind as I tried to find closure for this mess.\n\nThe letter had been delivered to me like a puzzle that couldn't be solved. The package was sealed and labeled \"gift\", but it was never opened.\n\nThis wasn’t your typical Christmas present scenario - there were no wrapping papers or glittery ribbons surrounding its contents. There was just plain white paper with a small tag attached at the bottom stating “Gift Unopened”. It read:\n\n“Dear [Your Name],\n\nPlease accept this gift as a reminder of how much you meant to us during those holidays last year when we needed comfort most.”\n\nIt seemed so simple, yet somehow everything else felt out of place once more. When they asked what happened behind closed doors (", "B": "I found myself in an alley, the sound of rain on asphalt echoing through my mind as I tried to find closure for this mess.\n\nThe letter had been delivered to me like a puzzle that couldn't be solved. The package was sealed and labeled \"gift,\" but it was never opened.\n\nAs I stood there staring at the empty space left by the gift, thoughts started pouring into my head. They were all mixed up together- they felt just right where each one belonged.\n\nIt took years before I ever saw them again; I thought they would disappear forever too with their homecoming. So when I finally did get back in touch with those who gave these gifts... well it wasn’t exactly what I expected at first sight...\n\nBut then things got interesting! When they asked what we could do next (", "C": "I found myself in an alleyway, not looking for any particular destination but simply wishing to be somewhere else for the moment. The air was thick with tension and there were people milling around that seemed intent on getting away from each other.\n\nThe city of New York had been so much more than I expected it would become when I arrived just over a week ago; everything felt wrong after leaving behind my comfortable life here in London where all went according to plan. And now here myself trying desperately to navigate through this unfamiliar landscape while also being wary as how best to get out safely before dark fell upon us completely unaware we even started walking toward this corner shop in Brooklyn Heights – one small wayfinding symbol amidst many others scattered throughout this vast metropolis meant to guide those lost or confused by directions alone wasn't sufficient", "form": null, "id": "p37", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a gift returned unopened"}
+{"A": "You hear your own name in the silence,\nand you know they are not real.\n\nIt is so sweet, it will always be with us,\neven though we never should have to say our names again.\nThis is what I learned today;\nI heard my name on the other side of the wall,\nI was called by my name but can't go back to this place where all my friends were gone,\nbut now I won’t ever leave and that’s why I’m okay.", "B": "You hear your own name in the silence,\nand you know they are not real.\n\nIt is so sweet, it will always be with us.\nWe love and feel for you, we don't think of you as a monster.\n\nYou'll never walk away from me again.\nThis isn't going to end, this will be alright if I say goodbye.", "C": "You hear your own name in the silence.\n\nThe air around you is tense, heavy and hot; you feel it against your skin like smoke from a cigarette burned too close to its tip. You look up at the ceiling of the empty room, but all that’s there is light—bright white lights scattered across the walls which are made out of concrete. This isn’t right. The place shouldn't be this bright; no one ever comes into this building except for maintenance personnel or", "form": null, "id": "p39", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about hearing your name through a wall"}
+{"A": "Verse 1:\nI thought I would find it at the foot of my bed,\nBut now I know, that you’re gone forever in me.\n\nChorus:\nCause I was so close to finding your body,\n\nVerse 2:\nAnd all these memories of when we used to play together,\nNow they just feel like empty air without you there to breathe.\n\nChorus:\nCause I was so close to finding your body,\nBut now I know, that you’re gone forever in me.\n\nVerse 3:\nAll those days where I could see you from behind the curtains,\nThey felt so real then but now they’ve faded away too.\n\nChorus:\nCause I was so close to finding your body,\nBut now I know, that you’re gone forever in me.\n\nVerse 4:\nHow do", "B": "Verse 1:\nI thought I would lose you all the time, but\nyou found your way home from me without warning,\nyour heart so full of pride,\nstill with aching love, how long will it take?\n\nChorus:\nYou are still my hope;\nif that is what we have to say goodbye;\nthen I'll be fine;\n\nWhat if you're gone?\nWe can't stay like this forever.\n\nVerse 2:\nThe moon's trying to tell us something;\nto hold each other while she goes up into the sky,\nthe stars shining bright in the night skies,\nthis world seems so lonely, do you know why?", "C": "Verse 1:\nI thought I would lose you\nAnd I would hide my eyes\nBut the truth is that we’re gonna get in trouble\nFor it’s love for a lost dog, who was on her own\n\nChorus:\nYou’ll find me when you come to know your heart\nWhen it’s all said and done\nLove is like this thing\nThat can go nowhere", "form": "verse/chorus", "id": "p48", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a lost dog; use a verse and chorus"}
+{"A": "The cold comes down, the ice forms;\nit is the snow that covers everything.\nIt melts away as soon as it appears,\nwhen spring arrives and all goes on.\n\nIf you like this, leave some feedback!", "B": "The cold comes quietly,  \nAnd brings with it the snow's sweet kiss.\n\nThe frost whispers secrets through branches bare,\nWinter's heart beats strong beneath its shell.\n\nAs time moves slowly on,  \nSnowflakes dance and fade away into dreams.\n\nSo let us cherish this brief season flown,\nFor Winter knows when to stay.", "C": "The cold comes down, the ice forms;\nit is the snow that covers everything.\nIt melts away as soon as it appears,\nwhen spring arrives and all goes on.\n\nIf you like this, leave some feedback!", "form": "single stanza", "id": "p49", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about winter in a single stanza"}
+{"A": "The first part of the refrain is a single line, the second one adds an extra syllable to each word,\nthe third one makes it further, and so on.\n\nThere’s nothing like the sound when you hold your hands behind your back, and then release them. \nIt feels good, but just for a moment.\n\nWhen I hear the voice mail after work from yesterday or today, if there isn’t anything urgent in it,\nthen I send it straight to my answering machine because it would be useless anyway.\nAs soon as we know what day it is, we start counting backward 10 days at once. It gets harder every time.\n\nYou can always have someone else tell you where they are going out for dinner. Even though sometimes it's easier to say it ourselves.\n\nJust look up at the sky with its stars above us and smile again without saying a thing.\nDo something silly like making fun of yourself or telling another person how stupid they are.\n\nThen forget everything for a little while and think how happy I am right now sitting here on this train station platform waiting for it to end before getting off into the distance where no one will find me except myself who has been waiting forever.", "B": "It’s always been like this\nYou’re in the same room\nBut you don’t know it yet\nWhen we were younger, I used to hold your hand,\nI’d say “Come on,” and now what do I hear?\nNow they call me by my name\nAs if you weren't there at all\nThat's how the world goes around\nThe words are always right\nAnd then, for no reason, when I was out with you\nI just lost my mind", "C": "It’s always been like this\nand it has to be that way\nNo matter what we do\nWe can’t change the time of day", "form": "refrain", "id": "p50", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about a voicemail that arrives too late; use a refrain"}
