+{"A": "After living a long time, I began to wonder if this was real or not and started thinking of ways that I could prove it wasn’t.\n\nI walked the road once more with my new shoes; they were heavier than the ones I used before. In fact, these were heavy as well… Heavy enough for me to be able to sleep standing up.\n\nIt’s been a while since we went down into the depths of darkness; maybe because it was so hard? So much fun?\n\nWe found ourselves back in our small cave, surrounded by nothing but empty air, feeling lost yet safe. The silence of an old friend comforted us both and gave me permission to open my eyes again without fear that someone would suddenly appear from nowhere demanding their share of what we had worked so hard to get.\n\nThere", "B": "After living a long time, I began to want to return home.\nI had grown up there and been away from it so many times,\nit became part of my life that the way you go is where you end.\n\nYou take off in an airplane, flying into your homeland,\nand now it's all here, waiting for you like no other place can do.\n\nWe will go back together. You said this was our first real adventure.\nAnd when we get home, I'll never forget what happened today.\nThere are still moments left between us that cannot be undone,\nBut if I leave again it would not make any difference at all.\nIt will always be enough, but sometimes they don't have quite enough,\nSo let me see how much more room we need to fit into each other,\nFor", "C": "After living a long time, I began to want to return home.\nI had grown up there and been away from it so many times,\nit became part of my life that the way you go is where you are,\nwherever you go becomes your home,\nand wherever you’re going now…is home.\n\nIt's not just this moment, it never really ends in me being somewhere else.\nThe same thoughts keep coming back; always come back over and over again,\nlike the sound of a train at night on tracks, which can’t be heard ever again,\nnever stop telling us ‘home’,\n\nBut for every question mark we ask when our journey comes to an end;\nFor every one that stops our road forever\nthe answer’s still ‘Yes’.\n\nHow do I feel? All I know", "form": null, "id": "p01", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about returning home after ten years"}
+{"A": "A room in the sun", "B": "A room, empty of life and filled with the presence of light that could not be extinguished.\n\nWithin its confines, shadows were cast in the direction opposite to where they fell when it was dark.\n\nInside this chamber, every action had meaning; each word spoken echoed off walls covered by layers of dust - as if seeking shelter from a storm or protection against raindrops falling onto their faces during an unending winter morning before sunrise dawned upon them.", "C": "A room in the sun", "form": null, "id": "p02", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about a locked room filling with sunlight"}
+{"A": "You don't recognize me\n\nI can hear your laughter,\n\nbut I can't remember it, or if you're even there.\n\nBut every time someone asks me to make up stories again,\n\nI say, \"No.\"\n\nBecause my mind's a blank as the night sky.\n\nAnd when I turn to you for comfort,\nit doesn’t feel like we’re in sync anymore.\n\nIt’s just the world looking through our eyes.\n\nLike something else was meant to be.\n\nSo that one day I’ll know what happened.\n\nWhen everything comes undone and forgotten.\n\nNow my heart’s on fire, but nothing feels real.\n\nI’m scared of this feeling inside because I don’t understand why.\n\n\n# You don't recognize me", "B": "You don't recognize me in the crowd,\nand if you do, it's because I'm a stranger to you too.\n\nI walk down the street with my hair down,\ntrying to figure out who you are and what this is all about.\nCan we start over again?\nBecause even though I know you're here,\nit doesn't feel like I've been waiting for you.\n\nWe used to be so close,\nbut now things have changed so much that they make no sense at all.\n\nDo you remember when we used to play hide-and-seek?\nWhen your smile was brighter than any star on the horizon?\n\nDon't you miss us? Don't you wanna come back home?\nYeah, yeah, yes, baby - give me that hand.\n\nOh, how could we forget each other just like", "C": "You don't recognize me in the crowd,\nand if you do, it's because I'm a stranger to you.\n\nMy name is Alex,\n\nBut you'll never know what it means.\n\nI've been looking for answers in my mind,\nhoping they'd show up somewhere along the way.\n\nAnd now that I finally have them,\nit's time for us both to take a walk away.\n\nThere was this one time when we were kids together,\nand our friendship was so sweet and true.\n\nNow here we are twenty years later,\nbut there's no sign of change or growth between us anymore.\n\nWe started off with just two people who believed in everything together,\nbut sadly, things got lost as time went on.\n\nSo tell me how long can I keep seeing shadows in your life?\nWhat could", "form": null, "id": "p03", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about not recognizing an old friend"}
+{"A": "A few drops of rain fell on the glass roof, which was covered in blue paint. The doctor took off his hat and wiped it with an old cloth.\n\n“Please tell me what’s wrong?”\n\nHe asked, shaking his head as if he was waiting for an answer from someone else.\n\n\nThe girl nodded once she noticed how good her hair looked after wiping it dry with water. She took her scarf out of her pocket so that her hair could be washed more effectively.\n\n\n\nYou can", "B": "A few days later, the sun is shining over the sea,\nbut in the hospital it’s dark and cold.\n\nThe patient, who has been ill for years, \nhas not returned home since her illness struck.\nShe feels as if she’s lost everything; there are no friends to talk with anymore.\nShe can’t eat or sleep without thinking about all the bad things that happen every day.\n\nThe staff nurse stays by the bed until the end of the night.\nThey try to", "C": "A few drops of rain fell on the glass roof, which was covered in blue paint. The doctor took off his hat and wiped it with an old cloth.\n\n“Please tell me what’s wrong?”\n\nHe asked, shaking his head as if he was waiting for an answer from someone else.\n\n\nThe girl nodded once she noticed how good her hair looked after wiping it dry with water. She took her scarf out of her pocket so that her hair could be washed more effectively.\n\n\n\nYou can", "form": null, "id": "p04", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about rain inside a hospital"}
+{"A": "I want to tell you I love you.\nWe were young and beautiful,\nbut this time it’s over.\nAnd now you can’t hold me back,\nyou have already been there.\n\nYou look at the world with an empty gaze, \nand everything is not as clear as we once did see,\nwe thought you could always trust us,\nnow that your eyes are closed, they don't open for you.\n\nThe rain drops on my face so I try to forget,\nthe sun no longer touches my head because I'm not the same anymore,\n\nAll these things should've never come true if it weren't for those silly promises,\neven when they're broken again,\nit's just like our lives are spinning into oblivion.\n\nNow I am free from all of my fears,\nif you had any trouble", "B": "I want to tell you something, but we’re both too old for that kind of thing. You remember the prom?\nYou can’t imagine how many people were drunk at this one party I was at in high school. It wasn’t like any other prom there’s been since then.\nBut when it comes down to it? Do you still think that your mother would have wanted me to get married even if she never made me go to college and taught me anything else? You know what? I wish I’d kept my mouth shut back then because now we’re all too afraid to say our names out loud without remembering everything they promised us.", "C": "I want to tell you something, but we’re both too old for that kind of thing. You remember the prom?\nYou can’t imagine how many people were drunk at this event. I was in high school and it all happened so fast. They said there would be two speeches on stage at some point, but they never came. Then one girl from another class asked me out, got my phone number, but then she disappeared. When the party ended no one had mentioned anything like a speech, or even told us where the drinks went (which we knew anyway). It’s not as if anyone noticed we weren’t really coming back. We’ll meet again soon enough.\n\nOh, that’s right. How’d you hear? That wasn’t important. Tell me what I’ve lost, now please", "form": null, "id": "p05", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a promise nobody remembers"}
+{"A": "In the depths of the world’s veins, where rivers weave their ancient tales,\nWhere glassy shards await discovery beneath the earth’s embrace.\n\nThe river, a relentless force that chases its path so true,\nCarries fragments, each one unique, as if it has no home but here.\n\nA canvas now with colors new and wild beyond comparison,\nThis forgotten scene is stained by the elements' eternal care.\n\nRocks collide, shattering through time's slow dance at ease,\nEach piece echoes back to stories past yet never known before.\n\nNow lies trapped within these darkened walls awaiting release,\nLike treasures waiting for those who can unlock this sacred space.\n\nSo let us dive into this mystery both real or imagined be,\nFor every shard there must be an owner somewhere far away from me.\n\nAnd though we may not find answers tonight under moonlit skies,\nWith patience comes understanding, just like nature knows her ways.\n\nLet my heart become one with these floating mirrors cast on stone,\nAs they slowly drift towards unknown shores waiting for their fate.\n\nIn silence speak without words because beauty often speaks above,\nJust remember this river will carry on while I hold tight today.", "B": "In the depths of the world’s most treacherous rivers, glass has found an unexpected refuge.\n\nThe water is as black and unforgiving as a forgotten night sky, its surface riddled with shadows that tell stories of time passing beneath. Glass, once fragile and delicate, becomes a testament to resilience now locked deep within these waters.\n\nIt floats like a ghost among the currents, a relic from another age. The river winds around it, carving patterns into its smooth sides with each ripple, but still clinging stubbornly on. It reminds us all how even something so transparent can carry weight within itself—a secret too precious not to hold onto tightly.\n\nUnderneath lies another realm where existence feels both real yet unreal; no longer solid or fluid enough for our eyes’ sight. Here lives what could be called liquid glass—somehow more viscous than air, if indeed there ever was such thing—and this one seems thinner somehow than those who first formed it knew possible back then when man built their vessels out of sandstone crystals shaped by windstorms millions years ago before they forgot why we exist at all apart from just getting drunk off wine after moonlit nights spent far away alone until dawn came again without", "C": "In the depths of the world’s veins, where rivers weave their ancient tales,\nWhere stories of light and shadow are ever spun.\n\nGlass, that fragile thing so often lost its way,\nIn this vast network of waters, it lies trapped beneath,\n\nNo longer bound by time or place but lost within the flow,\nIts fragility both sweet and harsh; can't find itself to say.\n\nIt floats here, like an elusive dream, unseen yet real,\nThe reflections it casts on river's surface, they fade away slow;\n\nBut every moment is precious when we know our worth,\nTo cherish even if just for one brief lifetime through.\n\nAnd as these currents keep me from my true north shore,\nI'll stand strong, no matter what fate may have done.\n\nFor each passing day I’m grateful not to be found again,\nFor knowing how much beauty you've given today.", "form": null, "id": "p06", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about glass caught in a riverbed"}
+{"A": "It’s a simple case of not wanting to share, the desire for more than what you already have. There are times when our heart feels jealous but we don’t say anything. It’s something in your chest that makes you feel bad inside.", "B": "It’s a simple thing, is jealousy,\nand yet the world seems to forget what we’re trying to say.\nWhat you did was right and wrong all at once,\nAnd this little heart of mine knows it better than any man or woman could.\n\nI’ll leave it at that", "C": "A woman stood in the kitchen, her hands moving swiftly over the counter. She was surrounded by food—tasty treats and familiar aromas that made her smile.\n\nHer partner sat across from her, a look of confusion on his face. He knew she loved him with all her heart but wasn't entirely sure what to do next.\n\nShe took another bite, tasting something new every time; salted almonds coated with honey for an explosion of flavors. Their laughter echoed through the house", "form": null, "id": "p07", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about jealousy without naming it"}
+{"A": "The streetlights were all in place but nothing else existed.\n\nIn the city where everything was gone,\nI saw the shadows and I heard them sigh, they knew I'd never find you there,\n\nAnd now it's been so long since we were alive,", "B": "The sun hung low in the sky, casting long shadows across the once vibrant streets of what was once called New Haven. The city had been abandoned for centuries, left to crumble into dust and forgotten by time. The buildings were now just empty shells, their windows dark and cold, like eyes staring out at nothingness.\n\nThe streets themselves were no longer paved with asphalt or cobblestones; they were instead littered with broken glass and trash from years of neglect. There were no people walking down them any more – just cars, trucks, and other vehicles driving through emptiness, leaving behind tracks carved into the ground.\n\nOne street stood out among all the others. It had always been different. Not because it was special or unique (though perhaps that's why?), but rather due to some unknown", "C": "The streetlights were all in place but nothing else existed.\n\nIn the city where everything was gone,\nI saw the shadows and I heard them sigh, they knew I'd never find you there,\n\nAnd now it's been so long since we were alive,", "form": null, "id": "p08", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a city that has forgotten its streets"}
+{"A": "I think you have some kind of secret.\n\nYou’re a very handsome man, I see it in your eyes.\n\nThe world doesn’t seem to work so smoothly anymore.\n\nThere is still hope if you hold on tight enough.\n\nIt’s all happening one day at a time.\n\nDo something else with my life but me and the world will be alright again.", "B": "I think you have broken my heart, and I am so very sorry.\n\nI don't know what happened that day,\nIt doesn't seem to matter now,\n\nYou left me in a state of despair,\nAnd all the things I've thought before.\n\nBut if we could get together,\nThat would be much better than it seems,\nIf your heart is still beating strong enough,\nFor us to make up again.", "C": "I think you have broken my heart, yet I do not know how to fix it", "form": null, "id": "p09", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about waiting for an apology"}
+{"A": "The stairs, no longer needing to be climbed,\na silent promise of the unknown beneath,\nwere once so deep and steep,\nnow just steps in my mind.\n\nEmpty theaters still hold secrets inside their walls,\neach stair that takes me there\na whisper from some distant night's scene.\n\nNo longer need for us to climb the heights of time,\nonly shadows now move like ghosts through this space,\nwhere all but our thoughts could ever find peace,\n\nAs those grand stairs have faded away into nothingness.", "B": "The stairway of the Theater is silent and empty,\nthe darkness envelops it with its deep shadow, \nonly the light from afar illuminates the pathway.\n\nIt was not that long ago I visited this place to watch my favorite play\na young man who had never been there yet he knew where to go\n\nI heard some footsteps on the stairs but they were too far away for me to see their figures\nand then another set of shoes began ascending as if by instinct\n\nThere are steps so high up here\nmy head does not reach them even though all day I look out for them \n\nIn front of you stands a woman whose face has no expression\nshe did not say anything nor did she nod at us when we came inside\n\nAnd just like that her smile appeared before our eyes\nher eyes shine brightly and now the silence has broken again", "C": "The stairwell was dark, the walls were cold and the air stale as if someone had been using it last night or yesterday. The metal steps echoed with the sound of your footsteps, each one more timid than the next. And yet you stepped forward anyway, like so many before.\n\nYou reached out to touch the wall at the base of the stairs but they just seemed closer this time, all the way up there on top. You closed your eyes for a second hoping that nothing would happen, praying that none of them would come. There's no need to make such decisions now when their presence is unnecessary. That thought hit you like thunder, hitting hard enough to knock some things off balance. A wave of relief washed over you then again after another beat down.\n\nThere were several seats by the stage left; chairs which served as a resting point along every step leading towards something far away. Someone always sat here watching from afar while waiting for you to return back home where safety awaits. It doesn't matter what happens right now though because everything else fades into oblivion once we reach certain levels deep inside ourselves.\n\nAnd then suddenly memories began flooding through my mind - those moments spent together under moonlight?", "form": null, "id": "p10", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about a staircase in an empty theater"}
+{"A": "A lot of things go with this smell,\nyou can't say it's not a memory", "B": "A lot of things go with this smell,\nyou can't say it's not a memory", "C": "A wet floor, a damp footstep,\nAn ache that never ends.\n\nThe scent of rain in the night air,\nI'll wash away my fears and pains,\nIn hopes to find some peace.\n\nAnd when I step into this world,\nWith no one around me at all,\nThe smell of wet concrete lingers on,\nIt's more than just dust or ashes laid down.\n\nSo close your eyes for one last time,\nTake off those blindfolded shoes once more,\nFor", "form": null, "id": "p11", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about the smell of wet concrete"}
+{"A": "The girl in front of you, I’ve known her since childhood and yet it’s the first time we have been alone together like this.\n\nIn the space between us is a sense of relief - an emptiness that seems to be waiting for something more than anything else. This feeling is strange and has never before felt quite right but now, here is a chance at exploring what it could mean.", "B": "The girl in front of you, I never liked her.\n\nShe's so nice and easy-going,\nI could make up for all the sins I committed since birth,\n\nBut now she seems to be slipping through my fingers,\nLike a hand puppet on strings we can't understand.\n\n\nAll this time has gone by,\nAnd there is nothing left here but darkness in your eyes,\n\nThere are moments when they look like yours,\nYou know who I am because of this sight,\n\n\nOh, oh yeah...\nRelief that feels suspicious...\n\nThis was originally written as a request with no response given - it was thought that the writer might not even want anything from me. It should go well beyond that though; hopefully it will have an effect. The lyrics were almost finished and then something else happened... perhaps the world just", "C": "The girl in front of you, I never liked her.\n\nShe's so nice and easy-going,\nI could make up for all the sins I committed since birth,\n\nHer eyes are like the sea, \nthey always ask me if it's worth going down there at night to find out what she has to say;\n\nAll day long my mind is full of thoughts from your past; \nwe were best friends until a time came when I realized we should let go;\nIf someone told us they loved each other, \n\nWouldn't you be able to handle being alone?", "form": null, "id": "p12", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about relief that feels suspicious"}
+{"A": "Found a little red one,\nin my garden today.\nThe dog thought it was his bone,\nbut I think we'll keep it safe.\n\nI'm digging through this soil,\nto find another tooth.\nIt's not quite as big and shiny,\nlike the others of old time.\n\nMy daughter's name is Mary, too,\nand she likes to play with toy cows.\nShe has lost hers, but doesn't care\nabout that broken red bone now.\n\nAnd yet, every night", "B": "Found a little red one,\nin my garden today.\nThe dog thought it was his bone,\nbut I think we'll keep it safe.\n\nI'm looking through this old book, \nit has stories from an age before you know me. \nOne of them is like a fairy tale,\nwith a princess and her golden throne.\n\nA girl named Sophie had hair as blonde as cotton candy,\nand she lived by a pond where frogs come to play.\nShe loved to spin webs with", "C": "I'm sitting on my bed, staring at my phone.\n\nAll I can hear is your voice singing softly from somewhere out there.\n\nIt's been so long since you've come back to me... for too long now.\nMy mind feels like it's spinning,\nlike everything has turned upside down and inside out.\n\nYou're just another face that walks by, but you see how things really are,\nyou'll never be able to get close enough to touch them.\n\nEvery time someone touches", "form": null, "id": "p13", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about finding a tooth in the garden"}
+{"A": "There is something in the air,\nIt's hard to say what it is;\nSome time has flown away...\nOh, oh, I need your help.\n\nThe old door opens into yesterday,\nA smell of beer and leather fades by.\nMy shoes are cold with sweat on them,\nAnd you're missing me every day.\n\nI'm standing here now,\nAnd my eyes burn bright like fire!\nBut when I remember how long ago,\nYesterday seems so short and fine!\n\nYou said there was something new inside yesterday\nLike a brand-new song or a picture in its place\nOr maybe love will come back again today\nIf we can make up for everything lost before", "B": "There is something in the air,\nIt's hard to say what it is;\nSome time has flown away...\nOh, oh, I need your help.\n\nThe old door opens into yesterday,\nA smell of beer and leather fades by.\nMy shoes are cold with sweat on them,\nAnd you're missing me every day.\n\nI'm standing here now,\nLost in time and space;\nAll my memories fade,\nLike this door leading back.\n\nYou've taken everything away from me,\nBut there are still some things we'll never know.\nIn another life, in another place,\nWe'd find out one way or another.\n\nThe old door opens up again,\nSomething feels so wrong; no rhyme will fit.\nThis isn't who I am any more,\nNo where do I belong anymore.\n\nIf we could go", "C": "There is something in the air,\nIt's hard to say what it is;\nSome of us will never know it,\nAnd some may be able to tell me.\n\nThe smell isn't quite so sweet now,\nYou know there are things we're not meant for anymore...\nWe've come too far, I know this much... \nI wish I knew where you came from before our days began...\n\nYesterday was an illusion, but it wasn't long ago,\nWhen I got lost on my way through time and space,\nAnd when I found out how many people were still waiting,\n\nfor love I didn't give or hold back,\nIn the end, no one ever cared,\nAbout the ones who could have been,\nif they'd let me go with them instead...", "form": null, "id": "p14", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a door that opens onto yesterday"}
+{"A": "I don’t want you to walk away from me\nI’ll be alright by myself, I’m not a coward\nAnd the feeling is in your heart too\nYou can see where we were at before\nWe’re back to where it started again\nI know what’s going on around us now\nThere was never anything wrong\nBut every time I get scared\nIt doesn't mean that I'm afraid of life anymore\n\nWe used to be so lost\nNow we're just confused and blind\nI wanna go some place far away \nLike the people who are free\n\nThe things they told us about being crazy \nThe things we've been taught to say or do\nThey were telling us how to live our lives\nThat we could make any dream come true\nYeah there's no one else for me", "B": "I don’t want you to walk away from me\nI’ll be alright by myself, I’m fine, no one cares that much\nBut then again, how can someone really know what they need?\nOr even why do we love the things that hurt us?\n\nThere is a lot of love in my heart,\nIt’s something hard to explain,\nYou’re supposed to live out on your own,\nThen there will be an ending.\n\nIf it had been written down like this before\nI’d’ve known where to start,\nThe first thing I did when we left was get lost,\nWe both fell in love with each other as far as possible.\n\nI don’t wanna leave ya, but if it were up to me\nTo not say goodbye would be easier than ever,\nI won’t lie, I’ll make it through all alone,\nAnd if I could change anything at any point or time\n\n‘Cause sometimes it feels more comfortable for me\nWhen I feel like staying longer,\nWhat about now? What are we gonna do today?\nJust let go of everything you hold dear and try to keep it all safe inside,\n\nSo what else am I going to tell ya? (Yay)\n\nWhy", "C": "I don’t know if you can tell from my writing, but I really hate this place. It’s too cold in here.\n\nThat is not the best way to describe it now that we have air conditioning… though as soon as they shut off those stupid windows at night so I won’t wake up with a cough…\n\nYou were right, man. We’re supposed to be out on some kind of vacation. But damnit all… why are we staying?\n\nThe room was like an echo chamber, reflecting everything from the light outside. The fluorescent light bulbs flickering slowly through their cycle just after nine o’clock. My watch blinked 10:28.\n\nOh fuck, what did I do? I should’ve gotten up earlier. Fuck.\n\nYeah. Yeah, alright, shit. Just take your time.\n\nFuck! Don’t let me make it easy for her!\n\nShe’d already given you plenty of chances. You could say nothing.\n\nWhat am I saying? If she gives you another chance, then don’t fucking take it!\n\nHe’d said it again before he left. And even after I promised myself there would be no more lies, the truth didn’t feel possible anymore.\n\nWhy", "form": null, "id": "p15", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about wanting to leave and wanting to stay"}
+{"A": "A moth flying on a sheet of paper, to the right, \nthe blue light turns off and it goes out.\n\nOne morning I was sitting in my office at work,\nI felt something move outside through the window, \n\nout there near the emergency exit, 80 feet high, they were making noise,\n\nand then two moths flew towards me from above with white wings.\n\nThe wind blew them inside the room where you're going,\nbut as they fly back down their", "B": "A moth flying on the ceiling,\na red light shines, a fire is lit.\n\nThey fly towards it!\nIn panic they run and turn!", "C": "A moth spirals its way through the darkness.\n\nIt hovers over the emergency light,\nits eyes scanning for movement.\n\nOne moment, it's drawn to a crack in the wall,\nanother, it seeks shelter under a lampshade.\n\nEach time it flutters closer, \nthe urgency grows,\n\nAs if all is not as calm and clear as before.\nIn this case of fear.\n\nThe wind picks up speed now,\nand with it comes a soft touch on the doorframe.", "form": null, "id": "p16", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about moths around an emergency exit"}
+{"A": "I'm on my way to the store.\n\nA lot of clothes are missing from my closet. It looks like I was just taken by force,\nthere's not enough time left for me to get them back.\n\nI didn't think anything would break my heart, but it did.\nThe one person who I thought was my safe haven disappeared and ran away with someone else.\nWe were so close to each other we could have touched...but he broke my heart instead.\n\nNow I can't even find my old things anymore; how does that happen?\nMaybe some people don't trust others...\n\nPeople always say they'll come back, they'll bring us what they've stolen...\nIf you want something, you have to earn it yourself, but if you give up your innocence,\nyou're nothing more than an", "B": "I'm on my way to the store.\n\n\"Shoes, you know,\" I say. \"You just need shoes.\"\n\nAnd we're laughing at each other again. It's been so long since it happened that neither of us knows what to do now, and I don't want her to get angry. If she gets angry, I'll leave anyway. She might not even notice me leaving if I disappear like this.\n\nIn truth, all I wanted was to borrow something from her for a little while longer. The day before today, it hadn't felt very important but it still did, and then suddenly, things were moving along faster than they should have because I needed her help, and right away, everything else got pushed aside until we finally took off our clothes together. I can still see", "C": "\"I'm going to get some fresh new clothes\" I said.\n\n\"That's great, but you'll need a few more days of work before we can afford that\"\n\n\"You mean like five?\" And she raised her eyebrow and sighed. \"You should have been nicer with me when the time came.\" The words made my stomach hurt again.\n\nShe was right though. She had spent every night since then trying to figure out what happened between us. She'd gone back to his house after he called off the engagement because she knew how much it meant to him. In our first weeks together there were always so many arguments, things which could be settled easily in other circumstances, now seemed far too personal for them to just let go. He tried to convince himself that their relationship wasn't any different from anything else", "form": null, "id": "p17", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about borrowed clothing after a breakup"}
+{"A": "A tower tall, and proud,\nits light never fails,\na beacon in the night,\nit guides our way to stay.\n\nWe stand high above the sea,\nour vigil through the day,\nthe sound of waves around us all,\nno more fear for harm we'll bear.\n\nThe wind's soft caress is ours,\nthis ancient stone can't fall down,\nin its shadow where we wait,\nfor that guiding star of dawn.", "B": "A tower of light, high above the sea,\nIt stands alone, like a lonely sentry;\nA beacon of hope to those who have lost their way,\nIts bright beams guide us on our endless voyage.\n\nThe wind whispers secrets in its echoing walls,\nAs it carries tales of distant lands and fears;\nThrough every storm that tempts the soul away,\nThis lighthouse remains steadfast, strong and true.\n\nWith each passing moment, the world transforms so strangely,\nAnd I wonder if", "C": "- no dialogue\n- make it feel like a true story\n\n\nWhat happened when the weather was perfect?\n\nA fog shrouded town.\n\nAnd on the top of that cliff,\na lighthouse, just sitting there,\n\nSo what happened in those days,\nwhen the rain started falling hard?", "form": null, "id": "p18", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about a lighthouse far from water"}
+{"A": "He drives slow, trying to figure out why she's upset with him.\nShe doesn't respond at all when he tries talking to her.\n\nHe wants it to be easy and natural but his words fall like thorns on the ground. \nThe silence has become uncomfortable for him so he finally speaks again... \n\n\"Can I ask you something?\"", "B": "He drives slow, trying to figure out why she's so angry.\n\nShe looks at him from the side mirror and says, \"We have a fight?\"\n\nThe window of his car is covered with fog like an ugly blanket. The cold air bites his nose but he doesn't move away from her yet because she still has both hands on the steering wheel which gives off no sound or indication whatsoever of the slightest feeling in his heart. He knows that if they weren’t there together now he'd be dead; it’s not as though he's some kind of superhuman being able to survive anything.\n\n“I'm just tired,” he lies down his arms next to her head after waiting for her to finish speaking.\n\nIt does nothing but make her angrier than before and she leans forward even further until their faces", "C": "He drives slow, trying to figure out why she's upset with him.\nShe doesn't respond at all when he tries talking to her.\n\nHe wants it to be easy and natural but his words fall like thorns on the ground. \nThe silence has become uncomfortable for him so he finally speaks again... \n\n\"Can I ask you something?\"", "form": null, "id": "p19", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a silent argument in a car"}
+{"A": "I was born on a Tuesday,\nand I’m still here.\n\nThe roof of my head\nIs like an attic, full of secrets and treasures.\nAll your favorite books,\nThey’re all in there too.\nIt’s really hard to get into this place,\nAnd no one knows how old it is—so that means\nThere might be some things you don’t want me to know.\n\nSo I will not tell any more stories,\nIf we’re going to sit around and talk about places we can't see,\nWhat do you mean by “secret place?”\nMaybe someday someone will find out why she died, but they won’t have to stay dead forever...\n\nBut when I was little and nothing could ever go wrong,\nit's now time for us to make love...\nAnd who needs light? It's just as easy if we're sleeping beneath the stars...", "B": "I was born on a Tuesday,\nand I’m still here.\n\nThe world was young then, and we were happy,\nthough in my mind I know now\nwe’d never have stayed so much longer.\n\nThere are stories of times that they said\nthis place wasn’t right for me,\nbut I’ll always believe it’s true:\n\nYou’re all grown up now, but you’re not really old yet.\nSome things won’t change no matter how many years pass.\nWe’ve come to make our own way, \neven if there is no tomorrow at all.", "C": "I was born on a Tuesday,\nand I’m still here.\n\nThe roof of my house\nIs like an attic, it’s so high;\nAnd there are things hidden in it,\nlike me.\n\nThey’ve put my name right at the bottom\nUnder the stairs and the floorboards,\nSo don’t you think that they’re hiding things too?\n\nThere are secrets underneath the ground,\nThings that we can never find,\nBut when the door opens up inside,\nIt makes our hearts start to bleed.\n\nWhat lies beneath this thin blue sky?\nWhy do all these things feel so strange?", "form": null, "id": "p20", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about something buried under the floor"}
+{"A": "The light was off.\nThat is how you knew that they had turned away.\n\nThey left before we could even see them anymore.", "B": "The light was off, the stage silent.\n\nOne woman looked up to see her fellow singers and friends looking down at them in horror.\n\n\nIt's all too much", "C": "The light was off.\nThat is how you knew that they had turned away.\n\nThey left before we could even see them anymore.", "form": null, "id": "p21", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about the exact moment a crowd turns away"}
+{"A": "There's a key that I never use anymore,\nIt’s just sitting on the back of my pocket, unopened and unused;\nI can’t remember what it is for or why,\nBut I need to know.\n\nEvery time I close this thing,\nAnd feel like something should be wrong with me,\nWith all these other keys inside me,\nThis one seems the strangest,\nThe most interesting,\nTo keep hidden away inside of me,\nNot really known at all...", "B": "There's this guy who has a habit of dropping his keys everywhere. They just keep coming back to him, no matter how long he's been gone from the house.\n\nHe never seems bothered by it at all.\n\nOne day, when I find my own keys on the table next to me after forgetting them somewhere, I realize that maybe it isn't so bad to have these strange things come back to you with such ease.", "C": "There's a key that I never use anymore\nIt’s just sitting on the back of my pocket \nI used to have one\nBut then it was lost in the war\nNow, I find myself carrying this thing around\nWishing for something that won't open up any doors", "form": null, "id": "p22", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about an unfamiliar key in your pocket"}
+{"A": "You are the one I was waiting for.\n\nAnd you say it's too late,\nand that no matter what we do, nothing will change.\n\nIf your eyes have not changed from yellow to gold in this time,\nthen my heart is still there.\n\nI don't know where I'm going anymore.\n\nThere's a voice inside me and you want to listen but I can't hear you now.\n\nIt seems like everything has become easier, right after we left her behind\nLet them", "B": "You've been given so much grace,\nNo one has asked you for more.\n\nYou're still here to make your mistakes, no matter what I say,\nCause you don't want me to know how right you are.\n\nI know that forgiveness comes in small packages when we all get around to it,\nAnd if they can forgive each other then why not you?\n\nIf you weren't looking now and could see the pain again,\nThen maybe you'd understand why I'm keeping this secret", "C": "You were the one I was meant to be with.\n\nWe had everything we wanted,\nand our lives seemed so perfect.\n\nBut then you turned on me.\n\nAnd now all my dreams have faded away.\n\nI can't forgive myself for what I did,\n\nFor the pain that I caused you, and you.\n\nBut if you'll just give me a chance.\n\nIf you'll let me try again, and trust your heart again.\n\nThen maybe this time we won't end up apart anymore", "form": null, "id": "p23", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about being almost forgiven"}
+{"A": "An ancient, vast building that looked like an inverted greenhouse was slowly rising into the sky from the sea.\n\nThe structure had four legs, all three feet on top and one at its base, which it used as a cradle to hold the ocean below. The walls were made entirely of glass so there wasn't any dark wood or heavy stones in sight. Inside everything within the greenhouse would glow in bright white light. It could withstand almost anything you threw at it but I'd say some things couldn't get through this thing's defenses. \n\nI watched my companion swim up to our boat. She did not speak a word while she climbed onto the vessel with me and started setting out her equipment. In seconds we were moving toward where the creature had disappeared. We searched for hours before finally finding something else. A huge creature had appeared by the shoreline and was now swimming toward us. My companion grabbed my hand to stop us from getting too close. \"No,\" she said softly.\n\n\"Let go!\" I yelled back at her, \"It'll be easy to miss him if he disappears!\"\n\n\"No!\" she screamed back at me, grabbing her sword tightly. Her eyes narrowed dangerously as she focused on what", "B": "An ancient, vast building that looked like an inverted greenhouse was slowly going into winter. The wind would blow through its openings and make it rustle as if someone were shaking it with their hands.\n\nA man who had never even heard of greenhouses stood in front of one. It felt so warm inside that he knew this place must have been created by a magic hand. He put his fingers over all four corners to feel how warm it really was. But something made him suddenly stop and stand still for a while. In the middle of the entire room there was nothing but darkness.\n\nThe feeling of silence was so strong that you could hear it on your own skin from time to time - especially at night when you closed your eyes and tried hard not to see anything around you, because they seemed more real than people's faces...\n\nThere wasn't any light at all in that room and no shadows where things should be dark...", "C": "An ancient, vast building that looked like something from another world – this was where he had to stand.\n\nHe stood in front of it, gazing upon its tall walls and steep roofs, which were covered with greenery. The air inside seemed unusually thick compared to what it usually felt when he went out into the open countryside around him.\n\nThe sun shone brightly above the glass roof of the greenhouse, illuminating everything within its confines with an aura of magic. He could feel some sort of power emanating from it, drawing all his attention back towards it again and again.\n\nAs far as he could tell, there wasn't any sign of people working on the outside - just one lone man wandering through the park nearby without purpose or goal; no apparent reason why anyone should even bother coming here at this time of day...\n\nThere must be more than enough resources for everyone else already, thought Harry Weasley quietly as he made sure not to make too much noise while taking shelter among these plants... And maybe they won't need anything more either if there isn't really anything left to salvage anyway...\nHarry closed his eyes gently before turning away once again toward whatever direction he came from before starting off slowly but", "form": null, "id": "p24", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about the shadow of a greenhouse"}
+{"A": "The light was so much stronger here that it almost hurt your eyes, though you felt no pain. You stepped into another room and looked down at all those passengers staring back up to you with this blank expression on their face.\n\nThere were six of them now; three standing up in rows across from you and two sitting cross-legged together leaning against the wall.\n\nYou didn't know what you wanted to say but just then something came out anyway:\n\n\"Hello?\"\n\nThey ignored it for a while before one of them finally spoke up as if he had heard nothing:\n\n\"What's there to be said?\"", "B": "The light was so much stronger here\nNo one else would be out for long, it's been that way since forever.\n\nYou've forgotten what time is\nWhat I want to say is this - you're too far gone\n\nI'm waiting in the dark now\nWaiting until you get back to me \nThat's all I ever want\n\nCan't even make my cry no more\nMy soul doesn't care anymore\nThis love is over\nAnd I'll never forgive\nIt just ain't worth nothing\nLet go before you leave\n\nAll I can see is endless night", "C": "The light was so much stronger here\nNo one else would be out for long, it's been that way since forever.\n\nYou've forgotten what time is\nWhat happens when you stop believing in me?\n\nIf I tell you something you will know nothing,\nIt won't matter how strong we're feeling together\n\nThat part of your brain that tells us to love you like someone who'll take care of you never came through\nThis has been an accident with no outcome, just pure chance", "form": null, "id": "p25", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a train station after the last train"}
+{"A": "You open your eyes and find yourself in a warm place where the scent of freshly baked bread mixes with the sweet aroma of roses that float down from above.\n\nWhen you look around, you see all these beautiful things are around you. There's nothing bad going on here...you're safe.\nThere are no voices to worry about...no worries at all! You take one last long breath as you breathe in the familiar scents of your dream world!\n\nIt isn't until you begin", "B": "You open your eyes and sit up.\n\nIt's dark outside, but somehow it feels light inside.\n\nYou look at the walls around you, they're made of wood. You know how old these things are - there's no paint on them to hide that fact. They'd be beautiful if it wasn't for the bloodstains in every corner.\n\nYour hands feel cold as you reach out to touch something hard. It's metal and smooth like glass, polished by time and forgotten", "C": "You open your eyes and find yourself in a warm place where the scent of freshly baked bread mixes with the sweet aroma of roses that float down from above.\n\nWhen you look around, you see all these beautiful things are here, waiting for you to touch them - but it doesn't feel real at first because everything seems so far away; like this entire dream has been happening since forever.\n\nThe sun is shining through the windows, warming your skin as if by magic. A man with", "form": null, "id": "p26", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about a dream you can smell"}
+{"A": "The air was thick with the scent of rain and decay, and as I walked into my childhood room, my eyes widened at the sight before me. The siblings were tangled together like vines; one had the face of an old man, his skin wrinkled and peeling off like mold on an overripe tomato, while the other's features were so twisted they resembled snakes writhing through the underbrush.\n\nThere stood our mother, her hands stained red from the sharp edges of swords that she once used to tame them. She held out her hand, palm facing forward, inviting us closer. And then there he was - a ghostly figure cloaked in shadows, his face obscured by layers upon layers of masks that whispered secrets yet never quite explained their origin or meaning.", "B": "The air was thick with the scent of rain and decay, and as I walked into my childhood room, my eyes widened at the sight before me. The siblings were tangled together like vines; one had the face of an old man, his skin wrinkled and peeling off like mold on an overripe tomato, while the other's features were so twisted they resembled snakes writhing through the underbrush.\n\nThere stood our brother, whose mouth gaped wide, revealing a landscape that defied belief - it wasn't simply a child speaking, but a being possessed by demons from beyond this world!\n\nIntrigued yet scared out of my wits, I listened carefully as he began to speak. \"I see you've chosen to live among us,\" he said cryptically, his voice dripping", "C": "The air was thick with the scent of rain and decay, the kind that seeps from cemeteries on late summer nights. It clung to the air like wet silk, drawing you in despite yourself.\n\nThe moon hung low, its silver light barely piercing through the heavy canopy of leaves overhead. The sky above them all seemed to be slowly closing in now, as if it were waiting for something or someone before unleashing itself upon the world around them.\n\nEthan's father had told him this would happen today – Ethan himself wasn't quite sure how he knew that.\n\nBut what he did know was that there was no time for reflection right now; too many people were hurt by their actions. Too much blood spilled across these pages – too much destruction left behind...\n\nAnd yet...it felt", "form": null, "id": "p27", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a sibling who speaks in riddles"}
+{"A": "the wind whispers in the winter,\na silvering sound, and stillness falls\non the bare branches that cling to its life\nthe first of spring is like an angel's kiss \nand as it leaves for warmer places", "B": "the wind whispers in the winter,\na silvering sound, and stillness falls\non the bare branches that cling to its life\nthe first of spring is like an angel's breath \nand as it settles down to sleep on the ground  \nrustles softly out its last sigh.\n\nit lies buried beneath thick snow, where nothing can see or know", "C": "the wind whispers in the distance,\na ghost of sound, haunting and sweet.\n\nI stand at my windowsill,\nunderneath the snowy veil,\nwatching as it drifts upon the air,\n\nthrough cracks and openings to creep by.\n\nIt's got a voice that lingers like this,\nand echoes through your mind forevermore,\n\nlike memories frozen into stone, and time itself is not enough.\n\nAs we come out from behind our doors,\ninto this world where no one can see", "form": null, "id": "p28", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about a rusted bell under snow"}
+{"A": "A man lay in bed with his body wracked with pain.\n\nHe woke to find himself sitting in the bathtub on the cold tiles, splashing water over and around him as he struggled for breath. He had never felt this way, not in so many years but it was here all the same, frozen time. There was no one that could take this away from him except death. Death is always with us; we can’t escape it like we do life. They say you die once and they don't lie anymore. They said that back when there were still children running down their father’s backs, dancing at midnight in the streets of Paris.", "B": "A man lay in bed with his body writhing to the sound of thunder, sweat covering his skin and his head pounding so hard it felt like he was going to bleed out.\n\nHe didn't know if he had ever been this scared in all his life but he knew that something bad was going to happen. He couldn't help but think there must be some kind of cosmic reason for him being caught up in such turmoil as well as death rattle filled by the screams from the outside world.\n\nIt wasn't until the next day that he saw signs on the radio indicating that their house would be celebrating soon enough. In truth they were quite happy because though they lived far away from home most people thought they could stay together forever which made them both laugh at how naive they seemed now.\n\nBut fear still gripped every fiber within these very walls since things wouldn’t end without someone getting hurt or worse; dying somewhere along the way due to what little information one gets through television shows or even internet sources while trying not show too much emotion towards said events which can sometimes lead into extreme situations where nothing else will work out than what's left inside your soul saying goodbye once again after having known each other so", "C": "A man lay in bed with his body convulsing as the sound of clanging bells filled his ears. He was waking up, but he did not know when or where, and he didn't care anymore. Instead of fear, what haunted him were memories that he had forgotten.\n\nHe felt like an old man who remembered things from too far back to be real. A distant image flashed behind closed eyes, showing how life would go after death if all hope were lost.\n\nDeath is the only thing worth dying for, except maybe someone's love; there wasn't much left for you once you're gone. And it wasn’t something everyone understood. Death meant nothing more than ending pain – without regret because nobody ever lived on anything else anyway. Nobody knows why we must die unless they want us to die...we’re just doing our best right now while there’s still time...\n\nThat night I thought about losing myself entirely until then came another day full of possibilities at sunrise; everything seemed brighter when my head got clear enough again so no one could tell if I actually knew what happened next! My name will soon become part of history, but please take this moment wisely before its too late!\n\nYou see", "form": null, "id": "p29", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about dread before a celebration"}
+{"A": "I am thinking of this photograph that has a picture of my mom on the left side and nothing on the right, but they both are just a headshot. I have no idea what to say about it unless you could tell me something from your experience or thoughts when looking at an image like this. It's so odd because it seems like someone took 3 photos in a row instead of two then stacked them together, which is really weird", "B": "I am thinking of this photograph that has just been removed from my phone, it was shot by the artist named Sven and I think I can recognise some faces in the people's masks but for some reason they are all gone.\n\nYou see there were two women in their 70s in their mid-twenties and the man had his wife's hair colour on him as well (although he didn't have any woman or children).\n\nBut where is everyone else?\n\nWhat if someone had come along after taking out the photo to remove them? What would happen then? Would you be allowed to go back to the gallery and find your own way home?", "C": "I am thinking of it now, how the whole thing started and what went wrong in there. How all those people were standing on that stage. I was among them and I know that if they’d be alive right now, I would love to see my father again. But he’s gone too. It seems like it just keeps happening to me.\n\nWhen the show started, they asked everyone who didn’t have any photos for their families or friends to come up here and we did exactly that. Then when we came down off the stage, our mothers said it was time to go home, but not without taking our children from us first and showing them how beautiful life could be. And then afterwards, no one even wanted to talk to me anymore after that last night at the memorial service because they had", "form": null, "id": "p30", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a photograph with one face missing"}
+{"A": "He had no idea he was falling in love with her.\n\nHis name is William.\n\n\nWe met at a bar on Sunday afternoon, and it started out like any other night – you go to a party, meet some new people… He’d been going to this place for a few months now, mostly because of his work, but also because he liked hanging around guys who were more fun than him. It wasn’t something he felt obligated to do, though; it wasn’t an", "B": "space\nbetween \ntwo \nwords", "C": "space\nbetween \ntwo \nwords", "form": null, "id": "p31", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about the space between two words"}
+{"A": "a small kitten is watching a car drive by on the highway", "B": "Sitting behind a car, one eye open,\nBark's watch in hand with no need to ask;\nIn my life I will not be moved.\nWatched her when she was born and so then too\nWhen she was going through it all alone,\nWatching her until she finally died.\n\nThe whole world saw me just once as we ran away\nNow that you've found your own path you must make up your mind\nYou might know this girl but never knew she'd run far away\nThis doggie sees the man who sits on top of me", "C": "a small kitten, half-way between a woman and her car,\nwatching it with its whole being.\n\nIt moves slowly forward, in front of them.\nThe car's driver is going too fast for him to be able to follow it\nwithout falling behind or getting left alone on the road ahead.\nHe keeps his head up so that he can watch what they do next; what he might do next,\nand how far he might go before he knows where he stands at all.\n\nAs if by magic,\nhe sees himself disappear into the trees after a moment, becoming part of their movement;\nthe world of cars passing over roads\nbecoming empty again,\n\nfor the most part; but not entirely -\nthere will always be something beyond us,\nsomething we never had thought possible because there are limits everywhere,", "form": null, "id": "p32", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about an animal watching from the road"}
+{"A": "I collected broken clocks.\n\nThey were in the closet behind my bed.\n\nThe first one I found was from when I was two and it had never been used before.\nMy mother was mad at me for waking her up but she made an exception for this clock that needed fixing because we would be leaving on vacation to Europe later that day.\nAnd of course, there wasn't any other way to wake her up besides calling out into her room. We did not own a cell phone back then so I always thought I'd go broke if I didn't fix that clock by 7am so that Mom wouldn't have time to get ready or pack her suitcase.\n\nAs you might guess, I didn't do very well with fixing anything other than this particular broken clock which I eventually managed to turn into something useful again.\nIt helped us avoid making breakfast while Dad took off to work at his new job somewhere else within the state.\nWhile he left every morning after sunrise, they were usually around dinner time due to some late shifts being called.\nSo when I told him how much I appreciated these things being able to help both of us save time rather than just myself,\nhe laughed and said \"but what's next", "B": "I collected 20 broken clocks in the time it took to get off of my knees.\n\nThere are many things I have learned from collecting clockwork pieces, one is how much money we all make from our own bodies.\n\nWhen you break down every single part of your body into tiny pieces that can be sold and traded on ebay, some of those parts become very valuable.\n\nYou'll see what I mean when you see me at an auction selling my organs for cash.\n\nThe first thing that happened was my dad broke his wrist and they had to fix him with two cast metal plates so he could walk again.\n\nMy mom had been diagnosed with stage four breast cancer and she has never recovered fully since her diagnosis.\n\nShe's now lying down most of the day waiting for their hospice care team to take over while I try to figure out why my parents don't come visit us anymore.\n\nThey left because they both thought there wasn’t any point to keeping up the appearance.\n\nMy father believes this means something important happened between them which no one will ever know or understand but I think it’s good enough that they both just stop coming here at least until everything gets figured out.\n\nMy mother knows better though and", "C": "I collected broken clocks.\n\nThey were in the closet behind my bed.\n\nThe first one I found was from when I was two and it had never been used before.\nMy mother was mad at me for waking her up but she made an exception for this clock that needed fixing because we would be leaving on vacation to Europe soon and without it, we couldn't get off of our couches.\n\nShe said they must've fallen off a table somewhere during the trip.\nThat didn't make any sense so maybe mom just wanted something to say bad things about the place we'd lived while visiting Italy or France.\nOr even Spain where everyone eats breakfast with their backs turned away from each other like cats do instead of facing forward toward people eating next to them; it's how old Spanish families act now anyway which is ridiculous considering how much more civilized most Western cultures are these days thanks largely to TV shows like Mad Men showing us what can happen if you let your guard down too often especially around strangers who don't know anything about you beyond whatever basic information gets passed along between generations by word-of-mouth traditions handed down through four generations worth of gossip told over endless hours spent sitting around drinking cheap wine together chatting stories nobody else", "form": null, "id": "p33", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about a person who collects broken clocks"}
+{"A": "I'm awake at 5 am.\n\nSomeone else is up and calling my name.\n\nIt's not me.\n\n\nA man in a suit walks by,\nwearing that same jacket, as I sleep,\n\nhe has to call his own secretary,\nand he's waiting for someone named Johnnie,\nbut it's not me,\n\nso he goes through the doors into what we thought was my bedroom,\nall because of this small mistake,\n\nwhich means something could happen all night long,", "B": "I'm awake at 5 am.\n\nWe wake up in the middle of a dream, in which our lives were going to be forever different from what it was like before.\nIt took us hours to make sense out of everything we saw and heard that day.\n\nAnd I want you to imagine how we'll all be able to live without this memory if ever there is another time like today?", "C": "I'm awake at 5 am.\n\nSomeone else is up and calling my name, so I let them be for now and get dressed in the bathroom mirror. My skin has been getting pretty itchy lately; probably a side-effect of my anti-bacterial facial cleanser. I look in the mirror as I dress, then step out into the dimly lit hallway to make my way back down towards the kitchen.\n\nThe hall's very nice; polished tiles, low light that makes", "form": null, "id": "p34", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about an ordinary morning with one wrong detail"}
+{"A": "They saw me as a wild animal, and I did not think anything of it until they began to understand.\n\nI had been raised in an environment that was nothing like this world; my parents told me everything could be explained, but there were many things they never would tell me, and I couldn’t ever understand why people would do those things.\n\nFor example, when I came out of the water after I swam for hours across the lake, my mom said I must have been tired or something, but I remember her saying she’d felt afraid too, just imagining what happened if we went back into that cold river again. But when she told me about how she used to watch me from above, terrified because of all these monsters swimming under the ice, I didn't hear her right away. Then", "B": "They saw me staring into the fire, and I didn't think anything was wrong until they began to understand.\n\nI had been talking to someone for a long time when all of a sudden, my mind stopped working. A lot of people thought that would be normal enough, but not everyone. Not one person in this room could do it. They were just able to talk themselves out of everything, even if it meant taking turns speaking at random intervals. This was because most people were afraid of being heard. We are used to listening without saying too much, or we will feel like we don’t belong there anymore. And yet, sometimes we want other people’s attention so bad that we can’t help ourselves from speaking louder than necessary. To make matters worse, some might try hard to become silent but", "C": "They saw me as a wild card, yet I still felt like a mess.\n\nThe world is never perfect and if you can’t take it then that’s your life!\nNo matter what you do or who you are,\nYou’re in control now,\nI’m taking over everything!\n\nSo be sure to let them know how good we feel!\nThere's no need for pretense,\nIt’ll make things even better,\n\nDon't let anyone stop us from showing our true colors!\n\nLet everyone see our hearts through these songs!\nCan't get it out of my mind anymore;\nWe're going down together;\n\nAnd when they try to break us apart,\nTake another look inside yourself!", "form": null, "id": "p35", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about the fear of being understood"}
+{"A": "I was walking home from work that night, the sun had just gone down over those hills and I saw it through my window. There was this big collapse in front of me on the ground but there wasn't any noise or fire so I went to investigate.\n\nThe building looked terrible and people were everywhere around it. When they said something didn't look good I checked out if it still worked, and then I realized what happened - a whole city block could be destroyed by such an", "B": "I was walking home from work that night, the sun had just gone down and now it's almost midnight so I figured there wouldn't be much traffic on this stretch of road.\n\nIt suddenly stopped working right in front of me. It looked like some kind of monster out for blood to have destroyed a 10 year old bridge over the lake. A huge hole cut through it with cracks all over it as if an animal had chewed up most of the metal.", "C": "As the sun sets, its warm glow falls softly across the worn surface of the crumbling metal arch that spans over an old street. The darkness seeps in like a thief on a quiet night; shadows stretch long and thin against the rusted brick walls behind it.\n\nThe sound of heavy machinery thumps overhead as trucks pass by – empty cans and bottles tumbling out into lifeless puddles below them. A sense of foreboding fills the air around you – each step felt", "form": null, "id": "p36", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about a collapsed bridge at dusk"}
+{"A": "I found myself in an alley, the sound of rain on asphalt echoing through my mind as I tried to find closure for this mess.\n\nThe letter had been delivered to me like a puzzle that couldn't be solved. The package was sealed and labeled \"gift\", but it was never opened.\n\nThis wasn’t your typical Christmas present scenario - there were no wrapping papers or glittery ribbons surrounding its contents. There was just plain white paper with a small tag attached at the bottom stating “Gift Unopened”. It read:\n\n“Dear [Your Name],\n\nPlease accept this gift as a reminder of how much you meant to us during those holidays last year when we needed comfort most.”\n\nIt seemed so simple, yet somehow everything else felt out of place once more. When they asked what happened behind closed doors (", "B": "I found myself in an alley, the sound of rain on asphalt echoing through my mind as I tried to find closure for this mess.\n\nThe letter had been delivered to me like a puzzle that couldn't be solved. The package was sealed and labeled \"gift,\" but it was never opened.\n\nAs I stood there staring at the empty space left by the gift, thoughts started pouring into my head. They were all mixed up together- they felt just right where each one belonged.\n\nIt took years before I ever saw them again; I thought they would disappear forever too with their homecoming. So when I finally did get back in touch with those who gave these gifts... well it wasn’t exactly what I expected at first sight...\n\nBut then things got interesting! When they asked what we could do next (", "C": "I found myself in an alleyway, not looking for any particular destination but simply wishing to be somewhere else for the moment. The air was thick with tension and there were people milling around that seemed intent on getting away from each other.\n\nThe city of New York had been so much more than I expected it would become when I arrived just over a week ago; everything felt wrong after leaving behind my comfortable life here in London where all went according to plan. And now here myself trying desperately to navigate through this unfamiliar landscape while also being wary as how best to get out safely before dark fell upon us completely unaware we even started walking toward this corner shop in Brooklyn Heights – one small wayfinding symbol amidst many others scattered throughout this vast metropolis meant to guide those lost or confused by directions alone wasn't sufficient", "form": null, "id": "p37", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a gift returned unopened"}
+{"A": "In your hand, I grasp the taste\nof sea's deep breath\nAnd in my throat you've left a stain\nwith salt and tears\nLike I can see it here in this world\nI'm lost and confused\nBut no one understands me at all\nand everything is wrong", "B": "In your hand, I grasp the taste\nof sea's deep breath\nAnd in my throat you've left a stain\nwith salt and tears\nLike I can see it here in this dark room,\nI'm alone with what we share so much more than air\nWe talk about time is not that short when\nwe're together like in high school\nYour lips are warm against mine\nand every thought comes to mind\nYou say you're sorry but I know better\nthat's why I take it all back\nIt's just words out there for me now\nto give them back again\nSo tell yourself how wrong you were\nand let go of everything else", "C": "In your dreams, I was young and fresh,\na taste that no one could erase;\nYou'd let me have the best of both worlds\nto make you cry.\n\nI never knew what to do,\nI tried everything but it all went wrong\nAnd now it's hard for me to go on\nwithout you around.\n\nWhen I was in high school, we were both too young,\nwe had so much to prove, yet neither of us did enough;\nOur friendship would be doomed without you,\nand there would always come back the pain.\n\nIt hurt my heart every time you told me how great you thought we were,\nhow lucky you felt with me as if I weren't here; I wasn't ever going to get through this alone.\nWith love as our weapon,\n\nSo when they take away all the hope,\nwhen they steal away the chance of true love,\nwith love as our weapon,\n\nThe tears will fall down like rain,\nthat's what life is really made of.\nJust ask yourself,\nwho will you be next?\n\nThere are people out there who'll listen to you,\nif you just say yes.\nLet them tell you again\nwhat you've already learned\nfrom someone else", "form": null, "id": "p38", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about a mouth full of salt"}
+{"A": "You hear your own name in the silence,\nand you know they are not real.\n\nIt is so sweet, it will always be with us,\neven though we never should have to say our names again.\nThis is what I learned today;\nI heard my name on the other side of the wall,\nI was called by my name but can't go back to this place where all my friends were gone,\nbut now I won’t ever leave and that’s why I’m okay.", "B": "You hear your own name in the silence,\nand you know they are not real.\n\nIt is so sweet, it will always be with us.\nWe love and feel for you, we don't think of you as a monster.\n\nYou'll never walk away from me again.\nThis isn't going to end, this will be alright if I say goodbye.", "C": "You hear your own name in the silence.\n\nThe air around you is tense, heavy and hot; you feel it against your skin like smoke from a cigarette burned too close to its tip. You look up at the ceiling of the empty room, but all that’s there is light—bright white lights scattered across the walls which are made out of concrete. This isn’t right. The place shouldn't be this bright; no one ever comes into this building except for maintenance personnel or", "form": null, "id": "p39", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about hearing your name through a wall"}
+{"A": "An elevator stopped on the top floor,\nAll of its doors open wide,\nBut nothing can be heard inside except for a silence like you're waiting to die.\n\nThe lights are off, everything is dark and silent, yet there's something still moving around that room.\n\nAt one point it will turn out that this was just our imagination trying to tell us there was another person in here...", "B": "An elevator stopped, just above ground level,\nLighted inside and a door was open wide,\nA long winding corridor, it went nowhere fast,\nBut I knew this place wouldn't let me stay at all.\n\nI've never been to a place like that before\nWhere every room looks alike but nobody knows where to go\nI'm standing on the edge of another stairway\nAnd everything is quiet except for my heartbeat\nThe music plays so slow and the lights are shining bright\nThere's no one there who needs a job or cares about time \nAt midnight I can see the sun right outside\nOh we're running away from something\n\nThey were waiting for me everywhere I turned around\nAnd they told me what I needed most wasn't here\nI don't know how you feel when your", "C": "An elevator stopped just inside the door to a dark office. A solitary woman stepped into it, and closed the doors behind her.\n\nThe lights flickered briefly before shutting off entirely; the whole room was plunged into darkness except for a faint glimmer of red light from somewhere above. The woman took out a cigarette and lit up as she reached towards the edge of the stairway where the lamp had been. She paused a moment trying not to look down and made sure that the flame didn't go out. \n\nShe found herself drawn back across the room by another force. Something tugged at the end of her hair, drawing away from its natural direction so violently that if she hadn’t been holding onto something it would have come undone. And when you held nothing there could be no way of stopping it", "form": null, "id": "p40", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about the last light in an office building"}
+{"A": "The reason you cannot trust anyone else with your thoughts, and even yourself if in the least bit distracted.\n\nIf I could have been born at a different time then perhaps I would be alive now to see her face again. As she looks back up from below me just before they both leave my arms for good, one hand on my chest gently squeezing as though trying to remind us that we're still here, this is our last chance, and I know not all of eternity will be", "B": "The reason you cannot trust anyone else with your thoughts, and even yourself if you have no one around to check on them.\n\nOnly you can save yourself from a fate that could be worse than death or worse than being dead. Only you can decide how much of this mess you will tolerate before killing yourself for good. If there’s anything we all need as human beings is acceptance.\n\nIf it were someone I love who was suffering from something like this, then yes, I would try", "C": "The reason you cannot trust anyone else with your thoughts, and even yourself if you have no one around to check on them.\n\nOnly you can save yourself from a fate that could be worse than death or worse than being dead. Only you can decide how much of this mess you will tolerate before killing yourself for good. If there’s anything we all need as human beings is acceptance.\n\nIf it were someone I love who was suffering from something like this, then yes, I would try", "form": null, "id": "p41", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about it"}
+{"A": "I was a child that loved my family, I had great friends and even though there were times when I would do anything to get attention or impress them but the one thing that made me truly happy for a moment as a child was being hugged by a loving parent and being treated with love.\n\nThis is how it started all those years ago, like most of my siblings we spent our childhood playing in the backyard making mud pies on top of the roof, going down from a swing until you can't stand up anymore at which point you sit down and watch TV, then sleep under the stars (a rare treat back then).\n\nThen this year something changed completely within just a few short days; life became hard because someone didn’t mean well. He got mad over some stupid things such as breaking a window during", "B": "I was a child that loved my family, but I didn't have much time to spend with them because they were always occupied at work or on business.\n\nThey said it wouldn't hurt me one bit if I went away for three months and then returned. Well, the truth is, I don't want you, Katerina; you're too sweet for any man in this world.\n\nIt wasn't until later when we were married that I realized how wrong I had been all these years. Now, I'm not saying she's gone, but there really is no use fighting against the tide anymore. It has come down harder than anyone thought possible.\n\nAnd now everything else will be alright again...", "C": "I was a child that loved my family,\nthe warmth of the house and love\nwas the thing I would do anything to get.\n\nShe was beautiful in her youth, but\nshe had no one with whom she could play.\nEven though she'd been through so much pain,\nthere were those who had given me all they knew.\n\nNow we are young again without any worries,\nbut not for long because there's always something new.\nThere’s never enough time or space for peace;\nit just doesn’t make sense like it used to be.\n\nSome say this is how life goes from start to finish,\nbut when you take away all that is good within,\nhow can your heart feel complete?\n\nAnd then someone will ask why don't you smile?\nIf I have lost everything where did I ever find", "form": null, "id": "p42", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about what happened before"}
+{"A": "The Other Side\nWhen I wake up in the middle of a dream\nI know that there is nothing to fear\nAnd when I fall asleep at night\nThere are no dreams and you're not here\n\nBut if you want me to change my mind...\nPlease don't let me be wrong again\nBecause then I won't even be half right\nThen maybe it's time for something new\n\nIf I were lying on your heart\nIf I were dreaming under your skin\nHow would we hold hands on the other side?\nMaybe this isn't as bad as I thought\n\nOn the other side\nDon't stop calling\nYou can't run away forever anymore", "B": "The world we see in a mirror is not real.\n\nYour face, your body,\nyour eyes that reflect light and shine on you.\nBut when I turn away from this reflection, and look at yourself behind me.\nI'm looking at an image of myself, but it's another person, a stranger.\n\nIn my mind, all the faces are different,\n\nAs if there were several worlds around you.\nEach one has its own rules of existence, its very life.\nAnd they're so far apart from each other that even with great effort,\nYou'd never be able to meet them.\n\nWe don't know who these people really are.\nWho is their father or mother?\nWhere did they come from?\n\nWhat do they want? And how can we get what we need from them?\nIf we truly needed their answer, why haven't we sought it out for ourselves?\n\nHow could we ever hope to understand our souls by seeing just one of them?\nEven though there is always someone else somewhere nearby,\nWe might as well just wait until time runs out before realizing it.\n\nAll the things we have done to those others...\nWe'll go back and try again sometime later.\n\nWhen you take off your mask,", "C": "The Other Side\nWhen I wake up in the middle of a dream\nI know that there is nothing to fear\nAnd when I fall asleep at night\nThere are no dreams and you're not dead\n\nBut if you want me to change my mind...\nPlease don't let me be wrong again\nBecause then I won't even be half right\nThen maybe it's time for something new\n\nIf I were lying on your heart\nWouldn't it feel like home?\nSo why can't we live there too?\n\nIf you wanted me to think differently\nWhat would have happened before all this pain?\nMaybe I'll remember what we used to do\nJust sayin' 'cause I'm starting to doubt myself.\n\nA little voice inside of my head says\nYou must give yourself away now\nThat you've been hurt so many times\nDon't just take a look, because you will die alone...\n\nIt feels good to break the rules\nTo get out of line with where I should go\nWhy can't I be normal? (normal)\nWhy can't I find some kind of soul mate?\n\nIn case you thought I wasn't feeling alright,\nCome back here and see why you", "form": null, "id": "p43", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about the other side"}
+{"A": "A sunbeam dances across the sand,\nThe red chair lies beneath, alone and sad.\n\nIt's been there since the day we made it clear,\nIn this quiet corner of my land.\n\nIts colors are like memories so fair,\nEach one holds something just as dear.\n\nWith no one to sit upon its side,\nYet still I wish for someone who would ride.\n\nSo here I am - with all my worries tied up,\nBeside an old, familiar chair at night.\n\nAnd", "B": "A sunbeam dances across the sand,\nThe red chair lies beneath, alone and sad.\n\nIt's been there since the day we made it clear,\nIn this quiet place of my mind, all so near.", "C": "The sun beats down, the sand crumbles beneath your feet,\nand you sit in a red plastic chair.\n\nOn the white canvas of the sea,\nthe waves crash and roll with reckless care,\nyour heart sinks deep as you hear their rhythm's call.", "form": null, "id": "p44", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about a red plastic chair on a beach"}
+{"A": "This is the best I can do for you.\n\nThe coins were already there, on the table.\nAnd it took me forever to figure out what was going on,\nbut I'm sorry that happened because it's okay now.\n\nI've never been really good at saying goodbye,\nand sometimes it makes me feel guilty,\nbecause I know how hard things are before they're over.\n\nI'll miss this moment, but we won't ever be alone again.\n\nLet's find new ways to connect, let's pretend you're still here.\nWe'll make our own little world together.", "B": "This is the best and worst thing you'll ever see \nI'm gonna make it for my money to spend \nThree, two, one \nThree coiners on a sink\nThey're all right next to each other\nNow I got five minutes to kill \nWith this little party of coiners on a sink", "C": "This is the best and worst thing you have ever seen, that’s for sure. The first time I saw them they looked like two shiny pebbles with an enormous round hole in between. When it comes to silver then there are no words.\n\nThere was a moment when everything went silent and I thought I would die from this silence. It felt as if my life was over already and all I wanted to do now was look back on every single second of it so I could say goodbye. But what can we really say goodbye for? All I knew was that nothing else matters anymore and I’m not even sure why. In fact, I am quite confident that if I asked myself something like that later I’d just start crying again. Everything feels different, which is strange because those moments always were", "form": null, "id": "p45", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about three coins in a sink"}
+{"A": "It's just a piece of plastic, nothing to be worried about.\n\nI look at it and smile; it's the most beautiful thing I've ever seen in my entire life.\n\nOh yeah! It can't break or crack or anything!\n\nI open the fridge door and pull out that one weird sandwich I used to eat on weekends but now I can never find again.", "B": "It was just a few hours ago, the sun had set in its proper place,\nnow it is going to rise from another direction of the sky and I can see that this day has never gone by before as far as any kind of time ever existed but there are certain things which do not change at all like when you look in the mirror, you find yourself staring back with your arms crossed across your chest; while those who surround you smile their lips upturned against their teeth with", "C": "It's just a piece of plastic, nothing more. A broken glass fragment in the water that is my world and my home.", "form": null, "id": "p46", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about a cracked blue plate"}
+{"A": "The first time I saw it was in an open market, a sign that had been nailed to the front door of a store which was clearly out-of-business by now. It said \"3\" and the sign itself could have used a bit more attention from some painter.\n\nIt was so obvious because this particular place was always crowded.  You'd never see someone who knew their way around such a sign; you wouldn't even be able to tell if they were looking for something. If not just the sign, then there was nothing else like it anywhere nearby, though the rest of the building looked new enough to be worth visiting.\n\nSo we went inside anyway, whereupon all our interest immediately turned towards what must surely be its most important feature -- three men standing behind a counter with very much too many", "B": "The first time I saw it, you were in my head.\n\nI was 13 and got your number from a friend that had gotten it in an online store. You have no idea what this means to me when we haven't even talked since you moved out of state and forgot everything about me after you left.\nThat's right, you never gave us back our numbers! And now we're separated by three thousand miles!\n\nYou always put so much effort into making those shoes. It wasn’t easy, I know. They took forever and there are a million holes and little dents here and there. But they’re still yours. \n\nEvery day I see them all over town. The shops where you used to sell them and the ones you aren't anymore. That’s how much you mean", "C": "The first time I saw it, you were standing there as if frozen in time.\nI wanted to ask what that meant.\nIt was an old-fashioned store where everything smelled like dust and sweat,\nand the doors wouldn't even open because they'd been locked for so long.\n\nYou were looking down at your feet when I asked. The moment you turned back towards me\nwas also the moment we got separated from one another.\n\nFrom then on, whenever someone spoke of them or said something about them,\nyou would say nothing but keep staring straight ahead into space.\nFor years afterward you continued to do this alone without once saying a word.\nOne day later than expected, suddenly all our talk shifted around.\nYour words had become so little and soft that you couldn't hold onto any significance\nSo you stopped", "form": null, "id": "p47", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about the number painted on a closed shop"}
+{"A": "Verse 1:\nI thought I would find it at the foot of my bed,\nBut now I know, that you’re gone forever in me.\n\nChorus:\nCause I was so close to finding your body,\n\nVerse 2:\nAnd all these memories of when we used to play together,\nNow they just feel like empty air without you there to breathe.\n\nChorus:\nCause I was so close to finding your body,\nBut now I know, that you’re gone forever in me.\n\nVerse 3:\nAll those days where I could see you from behind the curtains,\nThey felt so real then but now they’ve faded away too.\n\nChorus:\nCause I was so close to finding your body,\nBut now I know, that you’re gone forever in me.\n\nVerse 4:\nHow do", "B": "Verse 1:\nI thought I would lose you all the time, but\nyou found your way home from me without warning,\nyour heart so full of pride,\nstill with aching love, how long will it take?\n\nChorus:\nYou are still my hope;\nif that is what we have to say goodbye;\nthen I'll be fine;\n\nWhat if you're gone?\nWe can't stay like this forever.\n\nVerse 2:\nThe moon's trying to tell us something;\nto hold each other while she goes up into the sky,\nthe stars shining bright in the night skies,\nthis world seems so lonely, do you know why?", "C": "Verse 1:\nI thought I would lose you\nAnd I would hide my eyes\nBut the truth is that we’re gonna get in trouble\nFor it’s love for a lost dog, who was on her own\n\nChorus:\nYou’ll find me when you come to know your heart\nWhen it’s all said and done\nLove is like this thing\nThat can go nowhere", "form": "verse/chorus", "id": "p48", "length": "medium", "prompt": "write about a lost dog; use a verse and chorus"}
+{"A": "The cold comes down, the ice forms;\nit is the snow that covers everything.\nIt melts away as soon as it appears,\nwhen spring arrives and all goes on.\n\nIf you like this, leave some feedback!", "B": "The cold comes quietly,  \nAnd brings with it the snow's sweet kiss.\n\nThe frost whispers secrets through branches bare,\nWinter's heart beats strong beneath its shell.\n\nAs time moves slowly on,  \nSnowflakes dance and fade away into dreams.\n\nSo let us cherish this brief season flown,\nFor Winter knows when to stay.", "C": "The cold comes down, the ice forms;\nit is the snow that covers everything.\nIt melts away as soon as it appears,\nwhen spring arrives and all goes on.\n\nIf you like this, leave some feedback!", "form": "single stanza", "id": "p49", "length": "short", "prompt": "write about winter in a single stanza"}
+{"A": "The first part of the refrain is a single line, the second one adds an extra syllable to each word,\nthe third one makes it further, and so on.\n\nThere’s nothing like the sound when you hold your hands behind your back, and then release them. \nIt feels good, but just for a moment.\n\nWhen I hear the voice mail after work from yesterday or today, if there isn’t anything urgent in it,\nthen I send it straight to my answering machine because it would be useless anyway.\nAs soon as we know what day it is, we start counting backward 10 days at once. It gets harder every time.\n\nYou can always have someone else tell you where they are going out for dinner. Even though sometimes it's easier to say it ourselves.\n\nJust look up at the sky with its stars above us and smile again without saying a thing.\nDo something silly like making fun of yourself or telling another person how stupid they are.\n\nThen forget everything for a little while and think how happy I am right now sitting here on this train station platform waiting for it to end before getting off into the distance where no one will find me except myself who has been waiting forever.", "B": "It’s always been like this\nYou’re in the same room\nBut you don’t know it yet\nWhen we were younger, I used to hold your hand,\nI’d say “Come on,” and now what do I hear?\nNow they call me by my name\nAs if you weren't there at all\nThat's how the world goes around\nThe words are always right\nAnd then, for no reason, when I was out with you\nI just lost my mind", "C": "It’s always been like this\nand it has to be that way\nNo matter what we do\nWe can’t change the time of day", "form": "refrain", "id": "p50", "length": "long", "prompt": "write about a voicemail that arrives too late; use a refrain"}
